<sn><md>### Basic syntax

```bash
magick [options] input [options] output
```

Example:

```bash
magick input.jpg output.png
```

You can also use:

```bash
magick input.jpg -resize 50% output.jpg
```
</md></sn><sn><md>### Get the complete built-in help

On your Ubuntu system, these are the most useful commands:

```bash
magick -help
```

More detailed:

```bash
magick --help
```

List all settings:

```bash
magick -list
```

List supported formats:

```bash
magick -list format
```

List available options:

```bash
magick -list option
```

List image properties:

```bash
magick -list
```

Version:

```bash
magick --version
```
</md></sn><sn><md>### Image information

Identify an image

```bash
magick identify image.jpg
```

More detailed:

```bash
magick identify -verbose image.jpg
```
Example information includes:

* Width
* Height
* Format
* File size
* Color space
* Compression
* Resolution
* Channels
* Metadata
</md></sn><sn><md>### Resize

Resize to specific dimensions

```bash
magick input.jpg -resize 800x600 output.jpg
```

Maintain aspect ratio

```bash
magick input.jpg -resize 800x output.jpg
```

```bash
magick input.jpg -resize x600 output.jpg
```

Percentage

```bash
magick input.jpg -resize 50% output.jpg
```

Force exact dimensions

```bash
magick input.jpg -resize 800x600! output.jpg
```

The `!` can distort the image.

</md></sn><sn><md>### Crop

```bash
magick input.jpg -crop 800x600+0+0 output.jpg
```

Crop from the center:

```bash
magick input.jpg -gravity center -crop 800x600+0+0 output.jpg
```

Remove the extra virtual canvas:

```bash
magick input.jpg -crop 800x600+0+0 +repage output.jpg
```
</md></sn><sn><md>### Quality / compression

For JPEG:

```bash
magick input.jpg -quality 80 output.jpg
```

Common values:

```text
100 = highest quality / largest file
90  = very high quality
80  = good quality
75  = good compression
60  = noticeable compression
40  = strong compression
```

For example:

```bash
magick input.jpg -quality 75 output.jpg
```
</md></sn><sn><md>### Remove metadata

This can significantly reduce some image files:

```bash
magick input.jpg -strip output.jpg
```

For compression, this is often useful:

```bash
magick input.jpg -strip -quality 80 output.jpg
```

`-strip` removes profiles and comments/metadata that ImageMagick can remove.
</md></sn><sn><md>### Convert formats

JPEG → PNG:

```bash
magick input.jpg output.png
```

PNG → JPEG:

```bash
magick input.png output.jpg
```

JPEG → WebP:

```bash
magick input.jpg output.webp
```

PNG → WebP:

```bash
magick input.png output.webp
```
</md></sn><sn><md>### WebP compression

```bash
magick input.jpg -quality 80 output.webp
```

For lossless WebP:

```bash
magick input.png -define webp:lossless=true output.webp
```
</md></sn><sn><md>### PNG compression

PNG doesn't use `-quality` in exactly the same way JPEG does.

Useful:

```bash
magick input.png -strip output.png
```

Reduce colors:

```bash
magick input.png -colors 256 output.png
```

More aggressive:

```bash
magick input.png -colors 128 output.png
```
</md></sn><sn><md>### Change image quality and size together

A useful compression command:

```bash
magick input.jpg \
    -resize 1920x \
    -strip \
    -quality 80 \
    output.jpg
```

This:

1. Limits width to 1920 px
2. Maintains aspect ratio
3. Removes metadata
4. Compresses JPEG to quality 80

</md></sn><sn><md>### Background

Make background white:

```bash
magick input.png -background white output.jpg
```

Flatten transparency:

```bash
magick input.png -background white -alpha remove -alpha off output.jpg
```

Transparent background:

```bash
magick input.jpg -transparent white output.png
```</md></sn><sn><md>### Add text

```bash
magick input.jpg \
    -gravity south \
    -pointsize 30 \
    -fill white \
    -annotate +0+20 "Hello" \
    output.jpg
```
</md></sn><sn><md>### Add watermark

```bash
magick input.jpg watermark.png \
    -gravity southeast \
    -composite \
    output.jpg
```
</md></sn><sn><md>### Thumbnail

```bash
magick input.jpg -thumbnail 300x300 thumbnail.jpg
```

This is useful because `-thumbnail` is optimized for creating thumbnails.
</md></sn><sn><md>### Extract frames from GIF

```bash
magick animation.gif frame.png
```

Or:

```bash
magick animation.gif frame-%03d.png
```
</md></sn><sn><md>### Create GIF

From multiple images:

```bash
magick *.jpg animation.gif
```

Control delay:

```bash
magick -delay 10 *.png animation.gif
```
</md></sn><sn><md>### Combine images

Horizontally:

```bash
magick +append image1.jpg image2.jpg output.jpg
```

Vertically:

```bash
magick -append image1.jpg image2.jpg output.jpg
```
</md></sn><sn><md>### Check output file size

Linux:

```bash
ls -lh output.jpg
```

Or:

```bash
du -h output.jpg
```

Compare:

```bash
ls -lh input.jpg output.jpg
```
</md></sn><sn><md>### Important options

Some of the most useful `magick` options are:

| Option                 | Purpose                    |
| ---------------------- | -------------------------- |
| `-resize`              | Resize image               |
| `-scale`               | Fast resizing              |
| `-thumbnail`           | Create thumbnail           |
| `-crop`                | Crop image                 |
| `-rotate`              | Rotate                     |
| `-flip`                | Vertical flip              |
| `-flop`                | Horizontal flip            |
| `-quality`             | Compression quality        |
| `-strip`               | Remove metadata            |
| `-format`              | Change output format       |
| `-colorspace`          | Change color space         |
| `-colors`              | Reduce colors              |
| `-blur`                | Blur                       |
| `-sharpen`             | Sharpen                    |
| `-brightness-contrast` | Adjust brightness/contrast |
| `-negate`              | Invert colors              |
| `-border`              | Add border                 |
| `-annotate`            | Add text                   |
| `-composite`           | Combine images             |
| `-gravity`             | Position elements          |
| `-background`          | Set background             |
| `-alpha`               | Control transparency       |
| `-extent`              | Change canvas              |
| `-density`             | Set image resolution       |
| `-strip`               | Remove metadata            |
| `-interlace`           | Set JPEG interlacing       |
| `-sampling-factor`     | JPEG chroma subsampling    |
| `-define`              | Format-specific settings   |
| `-flatten`             | Flatten layers             |
| `-layers`              | Process image layers       |
| `-threshold`           | Convert based on threshold |
| `-equalize`            | Histogram equalization     |
| `-gamma`               | Gamma correction           |
| `-level`               | Adjust image levels        |
</md></sn><sn><md>### Summary

* `magick` is the main ImageMagick image-processing command.
* `-resize` changes dimensions.
* `-quality` controls lossy compression such as JPEG/WebP.
* `-strip` removes metadata.
* `-crop`, `-rotate`, `-flip`, and `-flop` modify geometry.
* `-colorspace`, `-colors`, and `-negate` modify colors.
* `-blur`, `-sharpen`, `-brightness-contrast`, and `-gamma` modify appearance.
* `-composite`, `-annotate`, and `-gravity` are useful for watermarks/text.
* Bash loops allow `magick` to process hundreds or thousands of images automatically.
* `magick -help` and `magick -list` provide the complete options available in your installed version.
</md></sn>